   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} e^{-n} \\) using \\( f(x) = e^{-x} \\). Take the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Exp",
         -1
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = e^{-x} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Exp",
            [
               "Negate",
               "x"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} e^{-x} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} e^{-x} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = - e^{- x} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} e^{-x} \\, dx = e^{-1} - e^{- b} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Exp",
               -1
            ],
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Exp",
                  [
                     "Negate",
                     "b"
                  ]
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( e^{-1} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Exp",
            -1
         ]
      }
   ],
